═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
§1  VOTING BILL LIFECYCLE
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  Every governance action in the organism follows the Voting Bill lifecycle.
  No decision is made without a bill. No bill is executed without ratification.

  ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────┐
  │ PROPOSE  │───▶│  VOTE    │───▶│ RATIFY   │───▶│ EXECUTE  │───▶│ ARCHIVE  │
  └──────────┘    └──────────┘    └──────────┘    └──────────┘    └──────────┘
       │               │               │               │               │
   Any agent       Weighted by      Threshold met   TERMINAL 01     Immutable
   with standing   role + token     via consensus   authorizes      ledger entry
                   stake            engine          execution

  PHASE 1 — PROPOSE
    Any agent with governance standing may propose a bill.
    Standing is determined by role-engine: agents must hold a role with
    governance-core authority domain. Proposals are submitted as CPL
    intelligence contracts via the CPL Compiler (TERMINAL 04).

    Required fields:
      bill.id .............. Unique identifier (auto-generated)
      bill.proposer ........ Agent ID of the proposer
      bill.domain .......... Governance domain (security, memory, economy, etc.)
      bill.action .......... What the bill does if ratified
      bill.rationale ....... Why the proposer believes this is needed
      bill.impact .......... ORACULUM-predicted impact assessment
      bill.expiry .......... Time limit for voting (default: 3 φ-cycles)

  PHASE 2 — VOTE
    All agents with standing in the bill's domain cast votes.
    Votes are weighted by two factors:
      1. Role weight — from role-engine authority assignments
      2. Token stake — from TokenEconomy stake balances
    Combined weight: vote_weight = role_weight × (1 + log₂(token_stake))
    Confidence floor: votes below 0.72 confidence are discarded.
    Dissent is recorded, never discarded.

  PHASE 3 — RATIFY
    The consensus-engine evaluates all votes against the ratification
    threshold. Default threshold: 0.618 (φ-inverse). Domain-specific
    thresholds may be higher for critical domains (security: 0.85).
    ORACULUM provides a prediction confidence on the outcome before
    the final tally — if prediction diverges from result, a review
    flag is raised.

  PHASE 4 — EXECUTE
    Ratified bills are forwarded to TERMINAL 01 (Sovereign Command) for
    authorization. IMPERATOR AGI reviews and authorizes execution.
    Sovereign veto may be applied at this stage (see §4).
    Authorized bills are compiled by the CPL Compiler and deployed.

  PHASE 5 — ARCHIVE
    Executed bills are archived to the enforcement ledger as immutable
    records. Each archive entry includes the full voting record, dissent
    positions, ORACULUM prediction accuracy, and execution outcome.
    Archived bills cannot be modified. They can only be superseded by
    new bills that explicitly reference the archived bill.


═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
§2  TOKEN-WEIGHTED GOVERNANCE
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  Governance power is distributed across all 20 packages via token stakes.
  Each package contributes a governance voice proportional to its token
  allocation in the TokenEconomy.

  Token Governance Distribution:
  ┌───────────────────────────────┬────────┬──────────────────────────────┐
  │ Package                       │ Tokens │ Governance Weight            │
  ├───────────────────────────────┼────────┼──────────────────────────────┤
  │ organism-kernel               │  1000  │ 0.15 — core lifecycle        │
  │ medina-memory-sdk             │   800  │ 0.12 — memory operations     │
  │ consensus-engine              │   600  │ 0.09 — voting mechanics      │
  │ nova-encryption               │   600  │ 0.09 — security decisions    │
  │ governance-core               │   500  │ 0.08 — governance itself     │
  │ agent-signal                  │   400  │ 0.06 — signal routing        │
  │ role-engine                   │   400  │ 0.06 — role authority        │
  │ knowledge-graph               │   300  │ 0.05 — entity relations      │
  │ memory-palace                 │   300  │ 0.05 — spatial memory        │
  │ temporal-memory               │   300  │ 0.05 — time-anchored recall  │
  │ team-vault                    │   250  │ 0.04 — team memory tiers     │
  │ agent-incentive-service       │   200  │ 0.03 — incentive mechanics   │
  │ sandbox-layer                 │   200  │ 0.03 — isolation authority   │
  │ organism-contracts            │   200  │ 0.03 — contract governance   │
  │ living-document               │   150  │ 0.02 — document lifecycle    │
  │ sovereign-bus                 │   100  │ 0.015 — bus routing          │
  │ harmonic-compute              │   100  │ 0.015 — frequency ops        │
  │ frequency-engine              │   100  │ 0.015 — harmonic timing      │
  │ medina-translate              │    50  │ 0.005 — translation          │
  │ replay-engine                 │    50  │ 0.005 — replay authority     │
  │ ai-tools-marketplace          │    50  │ 0.005 — tool catalog         │
  └───────────────────────────────┴────────┴──────────────────────────────┘


═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
§3  INTELLIGENCE CONTRACT GOVERNANCE
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  CPL (Contract Programming Language) intelligence contracts serve as
  governance instruments. Every governance bill is compiled into a CPL
  contract before execution. Contracts are:

    BINDING .......... Once ratified, contracts are enforceable
    VERSIONED ........ Every amendment creates a new contract version
    AUDITABLE ........ VERIFICATOR can audit any contract at any time
    REVERSIBLE ....... Contracts can be revoked via a new bill
    TRANSPARENT ...... Contract source is readable by all agents with standing

  Contract Governance Flow:
    Bill → CPL Source → CPL Compiler → Compiled Contract → Deployment
                           │                                    │
                      VERIFICATOR                          TERMINAL 04
                      audits source                        deploys contract

  The 5 Transformers (TERMINAL 04) process governance contracts:
    Transformer-1 (Value) ........ Assesses governance value of the contract
    Transformer-2 (Risk) ......... Quantifies risk of contract execution
    Transformer-3 (Trust) ........ Evaluates trust implications
    Transformer-4 (Decay) ........ Projects long-term contract viability
    Transformer-5 (Incentive) .... Aligns contract with incentive structures


═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
§4  SOVEREIGN VETO AUTHORITY & OVERRIDE CHAINS
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  Sovereign veto is the organism's final safeguard. It exists to prevent
  the collective from making decisions that would destroy the organism.

  Override Chain (highest to lowest authority):
    1. FOUNDER VETO — ItsNotAILABS founder authority. Absolute.
    2. IMPERATOR OVERRIDE — TERMINAL 01 sovereign command. Near-absolute.
    3. ORACULUM ADVISORY — ALPHA β prediction-based veto recommendation.
    4. CUSTOS SECURITY HOLD — TERMINAL 05 security freeze.
    5. COUNCIL SUPERMAJORITY — 0.85+ threshold override.

  Veto Conditions:
    A sovereign veto is triggered when any of the following are detected:
      • Organism integrity risk above ORACULUM threshold (default: 0.75)
      • Security violation detected by CUSTOS AGI
      • Token economy destabilization predicted by Transformer-2 (Risk)
      • Self-modification proposal without VERIFICATOR audit
      • Cross-terminal governance conflict without CONSILIUM resolution

  Veto Process:
    1. Threat detected → CUSTOS or ORACULUM raises alert
    2. Alert routed to TERMINAL 01 via PRAEFECTUS (ALPHA α)
    3. IMPERATOR AGI evaluates — applies override or escalates to FOUNDER
    4. Veto decision logged to enforcement ledger
    5. Affected bill marked VETOED — cannot be re-proposed for 7 φ-cycles


═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
§5  ORACULUM IN GOVERNANCE — PREDICTIVE OVERSIGHT
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  ORACULUM AGI (ALPHA TERMINAL β) provides predictive governance:

  Before every vote:
    • Predicts the vote outcome with confidence interval
    • Models the impact of the bill on all 20 packages
    • Identifies agents whose votes are most pivotal
    • Flags potential unintended consequences

  After every vote:
    • Compares prediction to actual outcome
    • Calibrates prediction models based on divergence
    • Updates trust scores for all participating agents
    • Archives prediction accuracy to governance record

  ORACULUM Governance Uses (from ALPHA β 10 Active Uses):
    USE 01 — Governance outcome prediction before votes are cast
    USE 03 — Sovereign veto recommendation with confidence intervals
    USE 09 — Contract outcome modeling for CPL intelligence contracts


═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
§6  VERIFICATOR IN GOVERNANCE — COMPLIANCE CHECKING
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  VERIFICATOR (Solver III — Integrity Verifier) ensures governance
  compliance at every stage of the bill lifecycle.

  Compliance Checks:
    PROPOSE .... Verifies proposer has standing and bill format is valid
    VOTE ....... Verifies vote weights are correctly computed
    RATIFY ..... Verifies threshold was met without manipulation
    EXECUTE .... Verifies CPL contract matches ratified bill intent
    ARCHIVE .... Verifies archive entry is complete and immutable

  VERIFICATOR Audit Types:
    STRUCTURAL AUDIT ..... Does the bill conform to governance schema?
    SEMANTIC AUDIT ....... Does the bill's action match its rationale?
    ECONOMIC AUDIT ....... Does the bill's token impact stay within bounds?
    SECURITY AUDIT ....... Does the bill create any security risks?
    INTEGRITY AUDIT ...... Does the bill preserve organism integrity?


═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
§7  CROSS-TERMINAL GOVERNANCE COORDINATION
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  Governance decisions often affect multiple terminals. Cross-terminal
  coordination is managed by CONSILIUM AGI (TERMINAL 03) with routing
  support from PRAEFECTUS AGI (ALPHA α).

  Cross-Terminal Bill Flow:
    1. Bill proposed in TERMINAL 03 (governance home terminal)
    2. PRAEFECTUS routes impact notifications to affected terminals
    3. Each affected terminal's AGI submits an impact assessment
    4. Assessments aggregated by CONSILIUM AGI
    5. Vote proceeds with cross-terminal awareness
    6. Execution coordinated across all affected terminals

  Terminal Governance Domains:
    TERMINAL 01 .... Organism lifecycle, system health, sovereign authority
    TERMINAL 02 .... Memory policies, retention rules, decay parameters
    TERMINAL 03 .... Coordination rules, role definitions, voting mechanics
    TERMINAL 04 .... Token supply, contract rules, transformer parameters
    TERMINAL 05 .... Security policies, encryption standards, enforcement
    TERMINAL 06 .... Deployment targets, substrate selection, canister rules
    ALPHA α ........ Signal routing policies, mesh topology
    ALPHA β ........ Prediction thresholds, trust calibration parameters


═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
§8  SELF-GOVERNANCE — THE ORGANISM GOVERNING ITSELF
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  The organism governs itself through recursive governance — governance
  rules are themselves subject to governance. This creates a self-
  improving governance layer that evolves with the organism.

  Self-Governance Protocol:
    1. ARCHITECTUS (Solver I) proposes governance improvements
    2. VERIFICATOR (Solver III) audits the proposal for consistency
    3. ORACULUM (ALPHA β) predicts the impact on governance quality
    4. CONSILIUM AGI (TERMINAL 03) submits the proposal as a bill
    5. Normal bill lifecycle proceeds (propose → vote → ratify → execute)

  Governance Invariants (cannot be changed by any bill):
    • Sovereign veto authority cannot be removed
    • FOUNDER veto cannot be overridden by any governance action
    • SAEIS enforcement cannot be disabled by governance
    • Token governance weights must sum to 1.0
    • Dissent must always be recorded, never suppressed
    • Archived bills must remain immutable

  These invariants are hardcoded in the organism-kernel and enforced by
  CUSTOS AGI (TERMINAL 05). Any bill that attempts to modify an invariant
  is automatically rejected before entering the vote phase.

  Governance Health Metrics:
    [■■■■■■■■■■]  Bill throughput ............... HEALTHY
    [■■■■■■■■■■]  Vote participation ........... HEALTHY
    [■■■■■■■■■■]  Prediction accuracy .......... CALIBRATED
    [■■■■■■■■■■]  Compliance rate .............. 100%
    [■■■■■■■■■■]  Veto frequency ............... NOMINAL
    [■■■■■■■■■■]  Self-governance cycle ........ ACTIVE


